# Line endings: LF in the repository, and LF on checkout on every platform.
#
# Without this, the answer depends on each developer's core.autocrlf setting.
# On this Windows machine it is `true`, so checkouts rewrite every file to CRLF
# and the next person to touch one commits a whole-file diff that buries the
# actual change in it. Two people on different platforms editing the same files
# is exactly the situation here, and `git blame` stops being useful the first
# time it happens.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what a checkout
# produces, overriding autocrlf rather than trusting it to be set the same way
# everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files git must never inspect or convert. A PDF or PNG that gets line-ending
# normalisation applied to it is a corrupt PDF or PNG — and the label artwork
# in this repository is test data that has to stay byte-identical for the
# extraction tests to mean anything.
*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.pyc  binary

# Windows batch files are the one exception: cmd.exe requires CRLF, and a
# batch file with LF endings fails in ways that are hard to read.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF — the shebang line ends up with a
# trailing carriage return and the interpreter is not found.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are generated: collapse them in diffs and mark them so GitHub does
# not count them toward the repository's language statistics.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff
